{"id":"factory-pomo","title":"Factory Pomo","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Factory_Pomo","rev":239193,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Factory Pomo"]}," (sometimes stylized as ",{"t":"b","c":["Factory PoMo"]},") is an aesthetic that was prevalent from the late 1980s to mid-1990s. Emerging from the 80s-90s postmodern explosion launched by ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Design"],"id":"memphis-design"},", it was prominently featured in industrial and graphic design of the time. This aesthetic often overlapped with ",{"t":"a","c":["Early Cyber"],"id":"early-cyber"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Cyberdelia"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cyberdelia"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Silicon Dreams"],"id":"silicon-dreams"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Lite"],"id":"memphis-lite"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Decoplex"],"href":"https://cari.institute/aesthetics/decoplex"},", and ",{"t":"a","c":["Wacky Pomo"],"id":"wacky-pomo"},"."]}],"sections":[{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Factory Pomo was heavily influenced by ",{"t":"a","c":["emerging CAD computer graphics"],"id":"silicon-dreams"},", and combined PoMo ideas with early-to-mid 20th century visuals (i.e. ",{"t":"a","c":["Art Deco"],"id":"art-deco"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Constructivism"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Constructivism"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Bauhaus"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Bauhaus"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Raygun Gothic"],"id":"raygun-gothic"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Streamline Moderne"],"id":"streamline-moderne"},", WPA imagery). The resulting aesthetic typically featured futuro-industrial elements like gears and metal ridging, contrasting colors, a certain emphasis on symmetry often combined with inverting colors, and strong use of simple shapes."]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Movies"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Batman Forever"]}," (1995)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Batman and Robin"]}," (1997)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Metropolis"]}," (2002)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["TV Shows"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Beakman's World"]}," (1992–1997)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Bill Nye the Science Guy"]}," (1993–1998)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["ReBoot"]}," (1994–2001)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Bibleman"]}," (1995–2010)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["My Life as a Teenage Robot"]}," (2002-2009)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Animal Mechanicals"]}," (2007–2011)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Video Games"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Sonic the Hedgehog 1-3&K"]}," Japanese box art (1991-1994)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["SimCity 2000"]}," (1993)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Sonic CD"]}," (1993)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Sonic 3 & Knuckles"]}," in-game (1994)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Knuckles' Chaotix"]}," (1995)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Sonic the Hedgehog The Screen Saver"]}," (1996)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Rides"]},{"t":"p","c":["Theme park attractions of the 1990s often used the Factory Pomo aesthetic. One prominent example of Factory Pomo was ",{"t":"i","c":["New Tomorrowland"]},", which opened at Walt Disney World's Magic Kingdom park in December 1994. Located in the Tomorrowland section was ",{"t":"i","c":["The ExtraTERRORestrial Alien Encounter"]},". Disney would further capitalize on this in 1998 with DisneyQuest, a virtual reality entertainment center located in Walt Disney World's ",{"t":"i","c":["Downtown Disney"]},", with a second location in Chicago, Illinois. They also had the ",{"t":"i","c":["ESPN Zone"]}," dining and interactive experiences in major cities, The style can also be found at Meow Wolf’s ",{"t":"i","c":["Omega Mart"]},", particularly in its factory section."]},{"t":"p","c":["On the other side of the pond, Sega opened Sega World Sydney in Darling Harbour, Australia, and Sega World London at the Trocadero in London. Sega also partnered with ",{"t":"i","c":["DreamWorks"]}," to establish ",{"t":"i","c":["GameWorks"]},", a series of arcades found at major cities."]}]},{"ti